<Layout
  title="Guillotine vs V-Cut vs Punch: Which Cut and Why | Cigar Codex Guides"
  description="The three main cigar cutting styles, what each does to the draw and flavor, and how to pick."
>
  <div class="home-hero">
    <div class="cigar-hero__eyebrow">Buying Guide</div>
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">Guillotine vs V-Cut vs Punch: Which Cut and Why</h1>
  </div>

  <section class="section summary-review">
    <p>
      Every cut style opens the cap so you can draw smoke through the cigar — the difference is
      how much of the cap they remove, and how that shapes the draw.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Guillotine</h2>
    <p>
      A straight blade (or two, in a double-guillotine) slices straight across the cap, removing
      a flat disc. It's the most common cut for a reason: it works on nearly any ring gauge, opens
      a wide draw, and is forgiving of an imperfect cut angle. The main risk is cutting too deep
      and cracking the wrapper below the cap — a sharp blade and a confident, quick motion matter
      more than a slow, careful one here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">V-cut (wedge cut)</h2>
    <p>
      A V-shaped wedge is cut into the cap instead of removing it entirely, leaving a notch that
      concentrates the draw through a smaller opening. This tends to funnel flavor toward the
      center of the palate and works well on firm, tightly rolled cigars where a full guillotine
      cut can feel too open. The tradeoff: there's no fixing a bad V-cut — cut too deep and you
      can damage the cap with no way to recut, unlike a guillotine where you can occasionally take
      a second, deeper pass.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Punch</h2>
    <p>
      A small circular punch removes just a plug of the cap, leaving most of the cap structure
      intact. This gives the most restricted, concentrated draw of the three and is popular on
      larger ring gauge cigars where a full guillotine cut can feel airy. Because so little of the
      cap is removed, a punch is close to foolproof — but that same small opening can feel too
      tight on thinner vitolas.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Which one do you need?</h2>
    <p>
      A guillotine is the right default if you only own one cutter — it works across the widest
      range of cigars. Add a punch if you regularly smoke large ring gauges and want a tighter
      draw, or a V-cut if you want a more concentrated, "spotlight" draw on firmer cigars and are
      comfortable with a cut you can't redo.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browse <a href="/accessories/cutters/">cutters</a> by Codex Score, or see our
      <a href="/guides/best-cigar-cutters/">best cigar cutters</a> guide for specific picks.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
